      "description": "Medical Director, Street Medicine Program Phoenix, Office of Community Engagement & Rural Health | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Medical Director, Street Medicine Program Phoenix, Office of Community Engagement & Rural Health Phoenix, AZ, United States | req27534 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 11/2/2026 Back to Search Medical Director, Street Medicine Program Phoenix, Office of Community Engagement & Rural Health Posting Number req27534 Department COM Phx Family Commun&Prev Med Department Website Link College of Medicine-Phoenix Medical Sub-Speciality Location Greater Phoenix Area Address 475 N. 5th Street, Phoenix, AZ 85004 USA Position Highlights The University of Arizona College of Medicine – Phoenix is hiring a medical director for the Street Medicine Phoenix Program at 0.20 FTE. Street Medicine Phoenix (SMP) is a student-driven interprofessional health care and social justice team that brings together students and faculty from the University of Arizona, Arizona State University, Midwestern, Mayo Alix School of Medicine, Midwestern University and Creighton University. We provide health care and other needed services to individuals experiencing homelessness directly where they are, whether in shelters, encampments or on the streets. This position will report to the director of Service Learning in the Office of Community Engagement and Rural Health. The medical director for the Street Medicine Program will provide clinical, educational and strategic leadership within our established Street Medicine team. This role supports the delivery of equitable, patient-centered care to individuals experiencing homelessness and housing insecurity. The Medical director will oversee the assistant medical directors and work closely with the program coordinator, student leaders and community partners to advance innovative models of care, expand educational programming and contribute to scholarly and programmatic growth across a collaborative Arizona street medicine coalition of educational institutions.\nThe successful candidate will fulfill the responsibilities of a faculty member in an appropriate department, actively participate in teaching, clinical service and/or scholarly work, and must qualify for an appointment at the rank of assistant, associate or full professor. The University of Arizona College of Medicine – Phoenix anchors the 28-acre Phoenix Biosciences Core (PCB) campus in the heart of the Valley of the Sun. The College inspires and trains individuals to become exemplary physicians, scientists and leaders who are life-long learners and inquisitive scholars. The PBC campus embodies the University’s priorities of engagement, partnership, innovation, and synergy in its world-class academic and research initiatives, with clinical facilities throughout Greater Phoenix. Visa sponsorship is not available for this positions. The University of Arizona has been recognized for our innovative work-life programs. For more information about working at the University of Arizona and relocations services, please click here . Duties & Responsibilities Oversee the clinical care delivery within the street medicine program including the assistant medical directors. Provide leadership across an Arizona Street medicine coalition of educational institutions. Lead strategic planning and program expansion efforts. Develop and expand clinical rotations in street medicine for medical students, residents and fellows. Mentor learners in clinical, service and leadership roles within the program. Mentor and support student, resident and fellow research projects related to street medicine. Identify, develop and lead grant applications and funding opportunities to support program sustainability and growth. Minimum Qualifications Hold an MD or equivalent degree from an accredited institution. Have completed a residency program in their specialty. Be clinical practicing in a primary care specialty. Be eligible for unrestricted medical licensure in the State of Arizona. Have experience and competence caring for people who are unhoused. Have appropriate malpractice insurance. Preferred Qualifications Be board certified in specialty area if an associate or full professor. Demonstrate experience with medical education with a focus on educating at the UME level. Have experience mentoring students in research projects. Demonstrate outstanding interpersonal communication and professionalism skills. Rank To be Determined Tenure Information Non-Career-Track (NTE) FLSA Exempt Full Time/Part Time Part Time Number of Hours Worked per Week Varies Job FTE 0.20 Work Calendar Fiscal Job Category Faculty Benefits Eligible No Benefits Rate of Pay AAMC Public Schools for Rank and Specialty Compensation Type salary at 1.0 full-time equivalency (FTE) Type of criminal background check required: Name-based criminal background check (non-security sensitive) Number of Vacancies 1 Target Hire Date Expected End Date Contact Information for Candidates Office of Human Resources, Talent Acquisition talent@arizona.edu Open Date 11/2/2026 Open Until Filled Yes Documents Needed to Apply Curriculum Vitae (CV), Cover Letter, and One Additional Document Special Instructions to Applicant Application: The online application should be completed in its entirety. Blank or missed information may be considered an incomplete submission. CV, cover letter and one additional document required for to be considered for the position. One additional document will be professional references. Please include at least three professional references at your current rank or above, their contact information and the nature of your work with them. Faculty selected for this teaching opportunity will be required to obtain faculty status within the appropriate academic department. This will include obtaining letters of recommendation and review by the appropriate College review committee. Notice of Availability of the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report In compliance with the Jeanne Clery Campus Safety Act (Clery Act), each year the University of Arizona releases an Annual Security Report (ASR) for each of the University’s campuses. These reports disclose information including Clery crime statistics for the previous three calendar years and policies, procedures, and programs the University uses to keep students and employees safe, including how to report crimes or other emergencies and resources for crime victims. As a campus with residential housing facilities, the Main Campus ASR also includes a combined Annual Fire Safety report with information on fire statistics and fire safety systems, policies, and procedures. Paper copies of the Reports can be obtained by contacting the University Compliance Office at cleryact@arizona.edu. Apply Now Share Save Job © 2024 The Arizona Board of Regents on behalf of The University of Arizona Equal Opportunity Employer Vets/Disabled"
